### Multi-Device Coordination Firmware

To manage multiple I2C peripherals cleanly in C++, robotics firmware uses non-blocking polling loops with `millis()` rather than blocking delays. Examine this architectural skeleton:

```cpp
#include <Wire.h>
#include <LiquidCrystal_I2C.h>

// Hardware Addresses on Shared Bus
const byte LCD_ADDR = 0x27;
const byte RTC_ADDR = 0x68;

LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(LCD_ADDR, 16, 2);
unsigned long lastPollTime = 0;
const unsigned long POLL_INTERVAL_MS = 1000;

void setup() {
  Wire.begin();        // Join I2C bus as Controller
  Serial.begin(9600);

  lcd.init();
  lcd.backlight();
  lcd.setCursor(0, 0);
  lcd.print("MONSTER LAB BUS");
  lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
  lcd.print("MULTI-DEVICE OK");
}

void loop() {
  // Non-blocking timer: poll every 1000ms
  if (millis() - lastPollTime >= POLL_INTERVAL_MS) {
    lastPollTime = millis();

    // 1. Transaction A: Ping RTC at 0x68
    Wire.beginTransmission(RTC_ADDR);
    byte rtcStatus = Wire.endTransmission();

    // 2. Transaction B: Update LCD at 0x27
    lcd.setCursor(0, 1);
    if (rtcStatus == 0) {
      lcd.print("RTC ONLINE 0x68 ");
    } else {
      lcd.print("RTC OFFLINE ERR ");
    }
  }
}
```

### Code Deconstruction:
- `Wire.begin()`: Initializes the ATmega328P Two-Wire Interface (TWI) hardware on pins A4 and A5.
- `Wire.beginTransmission(RTC_ADDR)`: Prepares the internal hardware buffer with the 7-bit target address (`0x68`).
- `Wire.endTransmission()`: Executes the complete I2C frame over SDA/SCL, sending START, address, and reading the target ACK.
- `millis() - lastPollTime`: Ensures transactions happen at measured intervals without freezing CPU execution.

